<h2>On air</h2>
<p>The ring is drawn <strong>outside</strong> the dot with a <code>box-shadow</code>, so it cannot change the lockup's size and cannot move a letter by a pixel while it breathes. Use it on a site that is genuinely publishing something now — a live blog, a stream — and nowhere else; a light that is always on is not a light.</p>
{{#example center=true}}
<span class="im-logo im-logo-live" style="--im-logo-size: 3rem"><b class="im-logo-type">Im</b><i class="im-logo-dot"></i></span>
<span class="im-logo im-logo-mark im-logo-live" style="--im-logo-size: 3rem"><b class="im-logo-type">I</b><i class="im-logo-dot"></i></span>
{{/example}}

<h2>One knob</h2>
<p>Everything inside the lockup is in <code>em</code>, so a single length scales all of it — the type, the dot, the gap, the tile, its radius. Nothing else needs touching to put the logo in a 20px bar or on a 200px cover.</p>
{{#example center=true}}
<span class="im-logo" style="--im-logo-size: 1.25rem"><b class="im-logo-type">Im</b><i class="im-logo-dot"></i></span>
<span class="im-logo" style="--im-logo-size: 2rem"><b class="im-logo-type">Im</b><i class="im-logo-dot"></i></span>
<span class="im-logo" style="--im-logo-size: 3.25rem"><b class="im-logo-type">Im</b><i class="im-logo-dot"></i></span>
<span class="im-logo" style="--im-logo-size: 5rem"><b class="im-logo-type">Im</b><i class="im-logo-dot"></i></span>
{{/example}}
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th></th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>--im-logo-size</code></td><td>1.625rem</td><td>The height of the lockup. The only one you normally set.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-logo-dot</code></td><td>0.235em</td><td>The dot, against the type size.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-logo-gap</code></td><td>0.085em</td><td>Between the last letter and the dot. Tight: the dot belongs <em>to</em> the word.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-logo-drop</code></td><td>0.055em</td><td>How far the dot sits below the top of the letter's box — which lands it on the cap line. Where the browser can trim the box to the capital (<code>text-box-edge</code>) this is <code>0</code>, because then the top of the box <em>is</em> the cap line.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-logo-dot-color</code></td><td><code>--im-accent</code></td><td>Change it for one instance, not for the brand.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Why it is text</h2>
<p>A logo is usually a file. This one is markup, and that is worth the trade:</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>It is sharp at every size</strong>, on every screen, with no set of exports to keep in step.</li>
	<li><strong>It follows the theme.</strong> The letters are <code>currentColor</code>, so the wordmark is ink on white and white on near-black without a second file.</li>
	<li><strong>It weighs nothing</strong> — no request, no bytes, no layout shift while a picture loads.</li>
	<li><strong>It can be read.</strong> Selected, searched, translated, and read out once as a name rather than spelled out letter by letter: the letters are <code>aria-hidden</code> and the lockup carries one <code>aria-label</code>.</li>
	<li><strong>It is the typeface.</strong> The logo and the headlines are the same Geist, so the brand is one decision and not two.</li>
</ul>
<p>The one place it cannot be text is the <strong>favicon</strong>: a browser tab will not run CSS or wait for a webfont. <code>assets/brand/mark.svg</code> is the square drawn as shapes at the same proportions — the stem of the letter and the dot — thickened enough to survive 16px, with the tile flipping on <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> and the dot staying the accent in both.</p>
{{#example center=true title="assets/brand/mark.svg, at 16, 32 and 64px"}}
<img src="/assets/brand/mark.svg" alt="" width="16" height="16">
<img src="/assets/brand/mark.svg" alt="" width="32" height="32">
<img src="/assets/brand/mark.svg" alt="" width="64" height="64">
{{/example}}

<h2>Using it</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Keep the dot's clear space.</strong> Nothing within half the tile's width of the lockup — in a bar that means the logo is the first thing and the gap after it is at least <code>--im-logo-size / 2</code>.</li>
	<li><strong>One dot per page.</strong> The wordmark in the bar and the square in the footer is two recording lights; pick the lockup per place.</li>
	<li><strong>The dot is never "you are here".</strong> As a marker on a card or a button it means new, live or unread — see <a href="/components/card/#the-dot">the card</a>. The current page wears a light fill and a bolder label instead, everywhere in the system.</li>
	<li><strong>Do not put the wordmark next to the site's name.</strong> It <em>is</em> the name. The square is the one that takes a label beside it.</li>
	<li><strong>Do not recolour the letters.</strong> They are ink. If the logo needs to sit on a photograph, put it on a tile — that is what the square is for.</li>
	<li><strong>A Ghost site that has uploaded its own logo gets that</strong>, with the site's name beside it, which is what Ghost's settings expect. The lockup is the default, not an override.</li>
</ul>
